ded as possible differential per age-independent alarm"], "alarm_features": ["hypotension (67/43 mmHg) with compensatory tachycardia (135/min) and tachypnea (34/min)", "congenital anomaly features (upslanting palpebral fissures, epicanthal folds, single transverse palmar crease) suggesting chromosomal disorder"]}, "branches": {"B1": {"id": "B1", "label": "Anatomical Bowel Obstruction with Air-fluid Levels", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "expanded", "prior": 0.5533242822165898, "posterior": 0.6122814880507266, "danger": 0.9, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.5, "evidence_for": ["Bilious vomiting and NG tube use for decompression in a neonate are typical findings for anatomical obstruction with air-fluid levels. This supports the leading branch (B1) as the most likely explanation of these clinical actions."], "evidence_against": ["The uncomplicated antenatal history slightly reduces suspicion for chromosomal origin of physical features but does not exclude it. Maternal refusal of testing leaves this as a potential contributor requiring definitive confirmation.", "The evacuation of stool via rectal exam provides the strongest single argument against high intestinal obstruction requiring NG decompression (e.g., duodenal atresia). This introduces minor doubt for B1 but does not overrule it."], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": ["B1.1", "B1.2", "B1.3"], "closure_reason": "", "reopen_triggers": [], "askable_discriminators": ["Are there radiographic signs of duodenal atresia (double bubble sign)?", "Is malrotation a radiographic finding in Figure A?"], "requestable_discriminators": ["Urgent abdominal ultrasound for malrotation", "Contrast-enhanced upper GI series to confirm duodenal atresia"], "turn_cost_to_refine": 3.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.8, "interrupt_relevance": 0.95, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "anatomy"}, "B2": {"id": "B2", "label": "Developmental Gut Motility Disorders", "level": 1, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.018959791445701386, "danger": 0.85, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["While NG decompression with NG insertion usually suggests higher obstructions, the rectal evacuation of stool implies the obstruction is not strictly proximal. This moderately weakens malrotation (B1.2) as a primary consideration."]}, "B3": {"id": "B3", "label": "Genetic/Chromosomal Anomalies with GI Manifestations", "level": 1, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.009052188229572133, "danger": 0.75, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["While NG decompression with NG insertion usually suggests higher obstructions, the rectal evacuation of stool implies the obstruction is not strictly proximal. This moderately weakens malrotation (B1.2) as a primary consideration."]}, "B4": {"id": "B4", "label": "Neonatal Functional Intestinal Obstruction", "level": 1, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.05175657160489633, "danger": 0.7, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Physical exam findings (upslanting fissures, epicanthals, and single transverse crease) are consistent with Down syndrome suspicion but could represent normal variation. Karyotype testing remains the definitive test needed to upgrade this to a specific diagnosis."]}, "B5": {"id": "B5", "label": "Neonatal Pyloric/Gastric Obstruction", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "reopened", "prior": 0.004370776998673397, "posterior": 0.00030493196136660583, "danger": 0.5, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": ["While NG decompression with NG insertion usually suggests higher obstructions, the rectal evacuation of stool implies the obstruction is not strictly proximal. This moderately weakens malrotation (B1.2) as a primary consideration.", "Rectal evacuation is atypical for pyloric stenosis, which lacks distal passage of stool/meconium. This provides strong evidence in favor of meconium ileus (B1.3) as a specific low-lying obstruction pattern."], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "reopen_triggers": [], "askable_discriminators": ["Is the vomiting non-bilious, especially with progressive force?", "Has there been weight gain with normal abdominal exam in prior feedings?"], "requestable_discriminators": ["Upper GI contrast to confirm pyloric hypertrophy", "Ultrasound for pyloric muscle thickness"], "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.4, "interrupt_relevance": 0.1, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "anatomy"}, "B1.1": {"id": "B1.1", "label": "Duodenal Atresia with Double Bubble Sign", "level": 2, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.008017925147070238, "danger": 0.85, "parent": "B1", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Rectal evacuation is atypical for pyloric stenosis, which lacks distal passage of stool/meconium. This provides strong evidence in favor of meconium ileus (B1.3) as a specific low-lying obstruction pattern."]}, "B1.2": {"id": "B1.2", "label": "Intestinal Malrotation with Midgut Volvulus", "level": 2, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.13668047124224894, "danger": 0.95, "parent": "B1", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["While NG decompression with NG insertion usually suggests higher obstructions, the rectal evacuation of stool implies the obstruction is not strictly proximal. This moderately weakens malrotation (B1.2) as a primary consideration."]}, "B1.3": {"id": "B1.3", "label": "Meconium Ileus with Cystic Fibrosis", "level": 2, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.46758309166140744, "danger": 0.8, "parent": "B1", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Rectal stool evacuation is more typical in malrotation with midgut volvulus than in cystic fibrosis-related ileus. This provides moderate support to malrotation (B1.2) while slightly weakening the case for meconium ileus."]}}, "frontier": ["B5"], "other_mass": 0.0, "pending_r